    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Hyperkalemia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " ECG changes are frequently visible once potassium is > 5.5 mEq/L."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Early hyperkalemia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " longer PR interval, shorter QT interval, and tall, symmetric, peaked T waves."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Potassium > 6.5 mEq/L:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " wider QRS, loss of the P wave, nodal and escape ventricular rhythms; finally a sine wave pattern, then ventricular fibrillation or asystole."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Hypokalemia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " cardiac effects are usually minimal until potassium is < 3 mEq/L."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Hypokalemia arrhythmias:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " premature ventricular and atrial beats, ventricular and supraventricular tachyarrhythmias, and second- or third-degree AV block."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Significant preexisting heart disease or digoxin:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " even mild hypokalemia can cause conduction abnormalities."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
